  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>AloSIM</strong> if you want a simple one-off eSIM with a bigger code (15%), a free second phone number through Hushed, and the option to buy fixed-GB or unlimited from one seller. Pick <strong>Ubigi</strong> if you visit the same country regularly and want a recurring plan, or you would rather buy from an established carrier-side company (Transatel, part of NTT Group). For a single cheap trip, check <a href="/airhub/">Airhub</a> or <a href="/redteago/">RedteaGO</a> first, since Ubigi in particular is rarely the lowest per-GB price.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: A Feature Bundle or a Plan Structure</h2>
  <p>
    AloSIM is built by AffinityClick Inc., an Ottawa-based team that launched it in 2022. Its angle is extras around an otherwise normal eSIM: a free international number through a partnership with Hushed (set up separately inside the AloSIM app after you buy a plan), and both fixed-data and unlimited plans on the same site. An unused AloSIM plan doesn't expire, so you can buy ahead and activate on arrival.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ubigi's angle is pedigree and plan type. It is a Transatel brand, and Transatel also supplies connectivity for connected cars. Beyond one-off plans, Ubigi sells <strong>recurring</strong> plans: a monthly plan that renews automatically and can't be cancelled for the first three months, and an annual plan paid for 12 months upfront that can't be cancelled or refunded during the year. Unused data doesn't roll over on any Ubigi plan.
  </p>

